No, tonight. First he didn't touch his dinner, and now he passes up hot chocolate.

232
00:13:23,200 --> 00:13:24,240
For Ben, that's quiet.

233
00:13:24,820 --> 00:13:25,820
Ah, he's fine.

234
00:13:25,860 --> 00:13:28,800
I saw him out in the driveway goofing off with Kenny just a couple of minutes ago.

235
00:13:30,880 --> 00:13:32,920
Ah, perfect. Yes.

236
00:13:32,920 --> 00:13:37,080
Dry ones. Now, you go like that.

237
00:13:37,800 --> 00:13:41,020
All right. Dant.

238
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:45,380
Now, that's a fire.

239
00:14:00,060 --> 00:14:04,460
Hi. I couldn't help but admire the pitch of your roof.

240
00:14:05,560 --> 00:14:07,200
Care to nibble on my muffins?

241
00:14:09,360 --> 00:14:11,100
Can I help you?

242
00:14:13,140 --> 00:14:14,160
Is Dwight here?

243
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:16,260
Oh, he's in the shower.

244
00:14:16,340 --> 00:14:17,140
Can I take a message?

245
00:14:18,300 --> 00:14:21,640
Well, roof, pitch,

246
00:14:22,360 --> 00:14:25,000
nibble, I hope you both...

247
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:30,300
Oh, what happened here?

248
00:14:30,980 --> 00:14:32,700
Carol made muffins for her new boyfriend.

249
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:34,720
Ah, Carol's got a new boyfriend.

250
00:14:35,100 --> 00:14:38,760
Ooh, muffins. So who's this new boyfriend of Carol's?

251
00:14:38,760 --> 00:14:41,740
Oh, you remember Dwight, the one I foxtrod with in the Catskill?

252
00:14:41,980 --> 00:14:43,660
Oh, the one who hits himself a lot.

253
00:14:44,120 --> 00:14:46,900
Mm -hmm. Well, honey, I've got a hunch this could be the one.

254
00:14:47,480 --> 00:14:50,540
I've never seen Carol act this way over a boy before.

255
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:55,700
Hey, morning, sweetheart.

256
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:03,200
So, tell me about this new boyfriend.

257
00:15:04,880 --> 00:15:07,380
Boyfriend? I have no boyfriend.

258
00:15:08,020 --> 00:15:09,160
I thought Dwight was...

259
00:15:09,160 --> 00:15:11,320
Dwight, please, give me a little credit.

260
00:15:11,400 --> 00:15:15,240
Since when does Carol Siever have to lower her standards for a fork -tongued

261
00:15:15,240 --> 00:15:18,700
medieval maggot? Sorry, my mistake.

262
00:15:18,740 --> 00:15:19,860
And another thing.

263
00:15:21,380 --> 00:15:25,580
As of this moment, Carol Siever does not live here anymore.

264
00:15:25,980 --> 00:15:27,960
Ich bin ein New Yorker.

265
00:15:29,960 --> 00:15:38,580
Jawohl. Carol, can I ask you something?

266
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:40,540
Ben, this is not a good time.

267
00:15:40,920 --> 00:15:41,720
Do you think I'm dumb?

268
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:45,120
Yes. Oh, it's a serious question.

269
00:15:45,620 --> 00:15:46,480
Do you think I'm stupid?

270
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:48,760
Ben, you're dumber than used chewing gum.

271
00:15:48,840 --> 00:15:51,240
You have the IQ of sawdust and you have your dryer set to fluff.

272
00:15:51,420 --> 00:15:53,860
If you need any more, I'll have to get back to you.

273
00:16:02,340 --> 00:16:03,140
Oh, I'm sorry.

274
00:16:03,140 --> 00:16:07,380
I'm sorry. Mr.

275
00:16:07,880 --> 00:16:10,640
Seaver, long time no see.

276
00:16:10,820 --> 00:16:13,100
I guess my rash was stress -related.

277
00:16:14,420 --> 00:16:15,600
Well, it's nice to see you, too.

278
00:16:15,680 --> 00:16:17,520
Why are you dressed like Chiquita Banana?

279
00:16:19,780 --> 00:16:24,340
Mr. Seaver, when you were under my tutelage, I was prohibited by law from inflicting

280
00:16:24,340 --> 00:16:28,720
bodily harm. Now that you've matriculated, I can wail the tar out of you.

281
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:31,260
Dad, it's for you.

282
00:16:32,420 --> 00:16:33,160
Mr. DeWitt, I'm sorry.

283
00:16:33,160 --> 00:16:34,900
What? What's wrong?

284
00:16:35,520 --> 00:16:38,440
And why are you dressed like some big banana?

285
00:16:40,440 --> 00:16:45,120
Oh, I was just cycling in the neighborhood and I thought I'd swing by to check on

286
00:16:45,120 --> 00:16:47,540
why Ben didn't apply for the advanced placement exam.

287
00:16:47,880 --> 00:16:49,060
Ben, he hates tests.

288
00:16:49,240 --> 00:16:50,620
He won't even look for Waldo.

289
00:16:53,260 --> 00:16:54,900
Just as I thought.

290
00:16:57,140 --> 00:16:59,520
This was obviously about smoke bombs and strippers.

291
00:17:00,820 --> 00:17:04,740
Obviously. Dad, where's the banana?

292
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:05,720
Went that -a -way.

293
00:17:06,400 --> 00:17:08,260
Great. Thanks. This could be worth big bucks.

294
00:17:10,160 --> 00:17:11,620
Hey, Ben! Yeah?

295
00:17:12,940 --> 00:17:14,580
Our principal, DeWitt, was just here.

296
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:17,280
Were you taking some advanced placement test?

297
00:17:17,760 --> 00:17:18,640
Yeah, right, Dad.

298
00:17:18,640 --> 00:17:19,480
What would I do with that?

299
00:17:19,680 --> 00:17:20,440
We both know him.

300
00:17:20,440 --> 00:17:23,360
No Einstein. What do you mean by that?

301
00:17:23,500 --> 00:17:24,920
What did you mean by it?

302
00:17:25,100 --> 00:17:26,820
I heard you when you told your accountant.

303
00:17:27,380 --> 00:17:29,000
Let's face it, Ben is no Einstein.

304
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:30,760
No Einstein? I didn't mean it that way, Ben.

305
00:17:30,760 --> 00:17:32,060
Yeah, well, you said it.

306
00:17:32,160 --> 00:17:34,520
And I know you meant it, because you didn't even know I was listening.

307
00:17:35,460 --> 00:17:37,800
You think I'm dumb and you've known me my whole life.

308
00:17:38,100 --> 00:17:39,280
I must be dumb.

309
00:17:39,880 --> 00:17:41,940
Oh, no, Ben...

310
00:17:45,140 --> 00:17:47,060
I have a confession to make.

311
00:17:48,760 --> 00:17:53,700
I didn't tell you before because I didn't want to appear all foolish in your eyes.

312
00:17:56,360 --> 00:17:58,960
It's my fault Ben's not taking that test.

313
00:17:58,960 --> 00:18:03,280
He expressed interest, and I scoffed, I belittled.

314
00:18:03,400 --> 00:18:04,620
I had a great time.

315
00:18:05,860 --> 00:18:08,580
I did everything but call him stupid.

316
00:18:08,980 --> 00:18:12,380
Oh. Since then, I've talked to his teachers.

317
00:18:13,160 --> 00:18:15,180
Ben has a 3.0 average.

318
00:18:15,480 --> 00:18:16,620
A 3.0?

319
00:18:16,620 --> 00:18:18,180
I know. Pigs are flying.

320
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:24,640
I have ruined your son.

321
00:18:25,420 --> 00:18:29,120
People trust me with their children, and I break them.

322
00:18:30,480 --> 00:18:32,860
Monday morning, I am turning in my resignation.

323
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:35,260
Mr. DeWitt, it's not your fault, it's mine.

324
00:18:35,420 --> 00:18:36,200
Oh, thank God.

325
00:18:37,500 --> 00:18:38,740
Oh, I think I almost quit.

326
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,240
Well, I guess I better get my schwinn in motion.

327
00:18:46,900 --> 00:18:50,100
Smile! Ah! That's a put -em -up!

328
00:18:53,540 --> 00:18:56,180
Hi, Carol. Hello?

329
00:18:57,400 --> 00:18:59,120
The strangest thing happened this morning.

330
00:18:59,460 --> 00:19:04,420
A woman in blue plaid came to my door, threw muffins at my sister, threatened her,

331
00:19:04,420 --> 00:19:05,500
and then ran.

332
00:19:07,640 --> 00:19:10,660
Your sister? Yeah, Elaine's a stewardess.

333
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:12,800
She visits whenever she flies into Kennedy.

334
00:19:12,980 --> 00:19:16,260
And my question is, why muffins?

335
00:19:19,060 --> 00:19:23,260
Um... You positively bloom at this hour of the morning.

336
00:19:23,900 --> 00:19:27,540
I do? I'd love for you to come meet Elaine.

337
00:19:27,720 --> 00:19:29,040
Want to come over for some muffins?

338
00:19:29,180 --> 00:19:31,180
They're not very good, but what the heck, they're free.

339
00:19:34,440 --> 00:19:37,360
Mustang Sally. Uh.

340
00:19:38,740 --> 00:19:44,520
Ha. Guess you better slow your mustang down.

341
00:19:45,760 --> 00:19:47,560
Oh, Lord. Mustang Sally.

342
00:19:47,640 --> 00:19:49,100
It's one of my all -time favorites.

343
00:19:55,460 --> 00:19:58,960
Ben, I had no business saying something like that, especially to a stranger.

344
00:19:59,400 --> 00:20:01,100
Yeah, right, and you didn't mean it anyway.

345
00:20:01,840 --> 00:20:03,720
No, I meant it.

346
00:20:04,060 --> 00:20:05,740
I really did think you were no Einstein.

347
00:20:06,380 --> 00:20:07,460
Thanks a lot, Dad.

348
00:20:09,680 --> 00:20:11,060
Neither was I.

349
00:20:15,680 --> 00:20:19,740
D, C, F, C minus D.

350
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:22,180
This is you?

351
00:20:22,880 --> 00:20:26,600
Mm -hmm. You really were no Einstein.

352
00:20:28,440 --> 00:20:29,820
You were barely trigger.

353
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:35,740
Point is, Ben, even Einstein was no Einstein.

354
00:20:35,840 --> 00:20:37,780
You know he dropped out of school?

355
00:20:37,980 --> 00:20:40,740
Some of us don't show our potential until later.

356
00:20:41,700 --> 00:20:44,440
So you're saying that someday I'll start showing my potential?

357
00:20:44,520 --> 00:20:46,060
You already have, Ben.

358
00:20:46,260 --> 00:20:48,640
Last year when you started studying at home with your mother.

359
00:20:49,200 --> 00:20:51,520
Yeah, well, I was fine when she was working with me.

360
00:20:51,700 --> 00:20:52,800
No, that's not true.

361
00:20:52,800 --> 00:20:54,360
Your mom gave you confidence.

362
00:20:54,460 --> 00:20:55,360
She got you started.

363
00:20:56,100 --> 00:20:58,100
But then you went out, you took over by yourself.

364
00:20:58,160 --> 00:21:00,300
You became a 3.0 student on your own.

365
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:02,560
I did, didn't I?

366
00:21:03,580 --> 00:21:06,180
And I almost blew the whole thing with that dumb remark.

367
00:21:07,060 --> 00:21:08,400
Well, then why'd you say that?

368
00:21:08,740 --> 00:21:10,720
Oh, you live in a family band.

369
00:21:10,760 --> 00:21:12,500
You tend to put labels on people.

370
00:21:12,620 --> 00:21:14,400
You know, Carol was the smart one.

371
00:21:14,980 --> 00:21:16,420
Mike was the charming one.

372
00:21:16,420 --> 00:21:18,960
And, uh, you...

373
00:21:18,960 --> 00:21:22,500
You're the one who goofy -glued stinky Sullivan to the doghouse.

374
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:28,120
You've gone through this whole change these last few months, Ben, and, well, it

375
00:21:28,120 --> 00:21:30,300
happened right under my nose, and I didn't even see it.

376
00:21:30,300 --> 00:21:31,160
I'm so sorry.

377
00:21:31,180 --> 00:21:31,860
I missed it.

378
00:21:31,860 --> 00:21:35,680
Please. I wish I could take back what I said, but I can't.

379
00:21:37,820 --> 00:21:39,140
Will you forgive me?

380
00:21:40,280 --> 00:21:44,240
I guess. We can start over.

381
00:21:44,640 --> 00:21:45,380
Clear the slate.

382
00:21:45,660 --> 00:21:47,840
Sure. All right.

383
00:21:50,240 --> 00:21:53,900
Hey, look, would you be interested in helping me input some data on the computer?

384
00:21:54,980 --> 00:21:58,660
You're kidding. You'd trust me to do something like that?

385
00:21:59,340 --> 00:22:00,140
Sure I would.

386
00:22:02,280 --> 00:22:04,160
Dad, this means so much coming from you.

387
00:22:08,660 --> 00:22:09,580
Will you pay me?

388
00:22:13,580 --> 00:22:14,360
I'll pay you.

389
00:22:14,560 --> 00:22:17,220
Oh, Dad, this means so much coming from you.

390
00:22:24,140 --> 00:22:29,460
On November 29th, 1991, Ben Siever arrived to take the New York State Advanced

391
00:22:29,460 --> 00:22:33,620
Placement Exam. Unfortunately, the test was scheduled for the 30th.

392
00:22:34,260 --> 00:22:38,000
Red -faced, the boy returned the next day and passed with flying colors.

393
00:22:38,600 --> 00:22:42,480
That same day, a smoke bomb was detonated in the faculty lounge.

394
00:22:42,480 --> 00:22:45,480
To date, no suspects have been apprehended.

395
00:23:00,220 --> 00:23:15,260
I'm going to go to the bathroom.
